.TH MU2E-POWER-RECOVERY 1 "2026-09-29" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.2.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-power-recovery \- assess, power on, verify and report on the Mu2e DAQ clusters after a power outage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-power-recovery
.RB [ \-\-phase
.IR PHASE ]
.RB [ \-\-execute ]
.RB [ \-\-simulate ]
.RB [ \-\-location
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-node
.IR HOST ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mu2e-power-recovery
drives the recovery of the Mu2e DAQ computing centres after a planned or
unplanned power outage. It runs from a workstation outside the DAQ networks:
nodes are reached over SSH through a gateway using Kerberos/GSSAPI, and BMCs
are reached by running
.BR ipmitool (1)
.I on
a gateway, because the IPMI subnets are not routable from off site.
.PP
The work is divided into four phases, which may be run together or separately.
.TP
.B 1. assess
A read-only survey. Every node's reachability, login, disk, mount, network and
power state is recorded. Nothing is changed. Its output is the "initial state"
report page.
.TP
.B 2. poweron
Nodes are brought up in the dependency order given by
.BR mu2edaq-power-sequence.yaml (5).
Each stage is verified before the next begins. Issues no power command unless
this invocation authorises it (see
.BR "LIVE RUNS" ).
With
.B \-\-node
only the named nodes are powered and the stages before them are verified,
never powered (see
.BR "PHASE 2 SCOPE" ).
.TP
.B 3. network
Node-to-node connectivity across the lab, data and IPMI segments, including a
jumbo-frame probe on the data network. This catches a switch that came back
with a missing VLAN or the wrong MTU, which leaves every node individually
healthy while the DAQ cannot move data.
.TP
.B 4. report
The consolidated narrative, assembled from the stored run after the run has
been finished, and optionally posted to the electronic logbook. It probes
nothing and can be re-run at any time (see
.BR "REPORT LIFECYCLE" ).
.PP
Before any phase, a run that will contact the cluster checks its GitHub remote
for a newer revision, fast-forwards if it is behind, rebuilds if the pull
touched a build input, and restarts itself. It then prints the version,
revision and configuration digest in force.
.B \-\-simulate
and
.B \-\-list\-checks
skip the check. See
.B PHASE 0
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Run control
.TP
.BI \-\-phase " PHASE"
One of
.BR assess ", " poweron ", " network ", " report " or " all .
Default:
.BR assess .
.TP
.BI \-\-label " TEXT"
Label for this recovery, shown in the report header and the logbook entry
subject. Defaults to
.IR user@host " " date .
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Authorise this invocation to issue power commands. Without it (or the
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ARM
token, see
.BR "LIVE RUNS" )
the run is a dry run: power states are read and the intended actions are
reported, but no chassis is switched on.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Contact nothing at all; answer every command from a built-in script. Use this
to rehearse the whole four-phase run, and to exercise the report, weeks before
an outage.
.B \-\-simulate
overrides
.BR \-\-execute .
.TP
.BI \-\-location " NAME"
Limit the run to one location:
.BR mc2 ", " mc1 " or " teststand
.RB ( heerc
is accepted as an alias). Repeatable. Defaults to
.I topology.locations
from the configuration.
.TP
.BI \-\-node " HOST"
Limit the run to these nodes. Repeatable. Short or fully-qualified names are
both accepted, as is a name that is not in the inventory (phases 1 and 3). A
name that is not a valid hostname exits 2 before any credential is acquired. In
phase 2 it bounds what is powered; see
.BR "PHASE 2 SCOPE" .
.TP
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
In phase 2, carry on to the next stage even when a stage does not meet its
requirement. Under
.B \-\-phase all
it also keeps the run going into the later phases after
.BR assess " or " poweron
has failed, which is otherwise where it stops.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " STAGE" ", " \-\-until " STAGE"
Start and stop the power-on sequence at these stage names, inclusive. Use
.B \-\-from
to resume a sequence that stopped, after fixing whatever stopped it. A name
that is not a stage, or a range whose end comes before its start, exits 2
before any credential is acquired and lists the valid stage names.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-failed
In phase 3, probe nodes that failed an earlier phase instead of skipping them.
.SS Credentials
.TP
.BI \-\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Kerberos principal for ordinary logins. If there is no usable ticket, a
password is prompted for and
.BR kinit (1)
is run into a private credential cache, which is destroyed when the run ends.
.TP
.BI \-\-root\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Kerberos principal with root access on the nodes. Kept in a separate credential
cache from the general one, so acquiring it cannot displace the other.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prompt
Never prompt for a password; fail with instructions instead. For unattended
runs.
.TP
.B service identities
The operator's own principal is
.I always
tried first, on every machine, for root sessions as much as for ordinary ones.
It is the identity the run belongs to, and no service credential is used where
a personal one would have done.
.IP
Only when a host refuses it do the tools try the Mu2e service identities \(em
.IR mu2edaq " and " mu2eshift
first, then every other identity in Vault \(em until one gets in or the list is
exhausted, returning to the personal principal for the next machine. Tickets
for these come from the
.B mu2edaq-kerberos
package, which owns the keytab-in-Vault layout; this project never handles a
keytab. See
.I kerberos.use_service_keytabs
in
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery.yaml (5).
.IP
For a root session the fallbacks keep the
.B root
login and change only the ticket: authenticating as
.I mu2edaq
and logging in to the root account is something a node's
.I root/.k5login
can authorise.
.I kerberos.root_fallback
turns that off.
.IP
The login name and the ticket are independent. The login comes from
.IR ssh.user " and " ssh.root_user ;
it is never derived from the principal. On these nodes
.IR mu2edaq " and " root
are the accounts that exist, and both are entered with the operator's own
ticket \(em an account named after the operator generally is not present.
.IP
Only an authentication failure advances the chain: a refused connection or a
timeout stops it, because a host that is down refuses every identity equally
and each attempt costs a full connect timeout. The report records which
identity opened each node. Nothing mutates the default credential cache or the
process environment.
.IP
A working credential is remembered per host, but only among the fallbacks: the
operator's own principal stays at the head of the chain for every host and is
never displaced by something that worked earlier.
.TP
.B credential caches
Tickets the run mints for service identities go into caches private to the run,
which are destroyed when it ends. On macOS the Kerberos implementation is
Heimdal, where a credential cache is a
.I collection
and each newly minted cache becomes the collection's default \(em so minting a
service ticket
.I displaces
the operator's personal ticket rather than destroying it. The tools record the
default cache's principal before every mint and restore the pointer after it,
and the service chain is abandoned outright if a restore actually fails, rather
than leaving the operator's shell pointing at a service identity.
.IP
The guard is a precondition. When the default cache cannot be read before a
mint \(em
.B klist
is missing, there is no default cache, or its output names no principal
\(em the mint is refused before
.B get-kerberos-ticket
runs, because a displacement could then be neither detected nor undone. Either
guard failure (unreadable before, or displaced and not restorable after)
abandons the service identities for the rest of the run: the run continues on
the operator credential alone, and the reason and what to do about it
.RB ( kinit ", or " "kswitch \-p" )
are recorded as a phase note and as an
.B error
event in the run store. The decision is made on the error's type, not its
wording.
.IP
One exception: when both
.B \-\-principal
and
.B \-\-root\-principal
are designated, the run uses only its own private caches and no default cache
at all, so a mint may proceed when klist reports that there is simply no
default (a fresh macOS login, for example). If the mint leaves its service
identity as the new default, that cache is destroyed by name; if it cannot be,
the service identities are abandoned as above. A missing or unparsable klist is
still refused. Acquiring the designated principals themselves is allowed when there is no
default at all (the operator's own principal becoming the default is not a
displacement); a default that exists but cannot be read is refused before the
password prompt \(em run
.B klist
to see why.
.IP
Minting is serialised. The service identities are minted once each, before any
worker thread starts, and every mint \(em including
.BR kinit (1)
of a designated principal \(em runs as one locked read/mint/restore
transaction, so two workers can neither mint the same identity twice nor
interleave their restores of the default cache.
.IP
A Heimdal cache has a
.I name
.RI ( API : uuid )
rather than a path, and Heimdal ignores
.B KRB5CCNAME=FILE:
for
.BR kinit (1),
so the identity is looked up in the collection and the name found there is what
the session is given. Caches are destroyed by name with
.BR kdestroy (1)
.BR \-c ,
and a file cache whose path is not inside the run's own temporary directory is
refused rather than destroyed.
.IP
Before any connection is attempted the run inspects the default cache. When it
holds a service identity instead of a personal principal \(em usually the
residue of an earlier interrupted run \(em the run
.I warns
and prints the line that fixes it. It is a warning only: the run continues into
every phase, so act on it rather than expecting to be stopped. The line is
.B "kswitch \-p"
.IR you @FNAL.GOV,
or
.B "kdestroy \-\-all"
followed by a fresh
.BR kinit (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-vault\-addr " URL"
HashiCorp Vault address. Default:
.IR https://ssivault.fnal.gov:8200 .
.SS Report
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Where the static report pages are written. Default:
.IR html/ .
.TP
.B \-\-publish
Copy the report to
.I report.publish.target
after the run.
.TP
.BI \-\-publish\-target " DEST"
Override the publication target, e.g.
.IR user@host:/web/recovery/ .
Implies
.BR \-\-publish .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-report
Do not write the HTML pages; console output only.
.TP
.B \-\-post\-ecl
Post the phase-4 report to the electronic logbook. Requires the
.B ecl-client
package and ECL credentials in Vault.
.TP
.BI \-\-run\-id " N"
Regenerate (and with
.BR \-\-post\-ecl ,
repost) the report of stored run
.IR N .
Valid only with
.BR "\-\-phase report" ;
with any other phase it is an error (exit 2), because it would mean reporting
on one run while recording another. A run id that is not in the store is a
usage error (exit 2): no run row is created and no credential is touched.
Without it, the report phase reports on the newest run.
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary, the worked examples and the exit status table, and
exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.I mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.2.0
and exit. The same flag is accepted by
.BR mu2e-power-state (1),
.BR mu2e-power-on (1),
.BR mu2e-power-netcheck (1)
and
.BR mu2e-power-report (1);
they all report the package version, not a per-command one.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Main configuration file. Default:
.IR config/power-recovery.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Dotenv file. Default:
.IR config/.env .
.TP
.BI \-\-database\-url " URL"
SQLAlchemy URL for the run store. Default: SQLite under
.IR data/ .
Set a
.I postgresql+psycopg2://
URL to use Postgres instead.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-self\-update
Skip the phase-0 check for a newer revision.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-checks
Print every registered check and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-nodes
Print the node inventory and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Reserve stdout for exactly one JSON document; every human-readable line
(banner, mode notice, phase tables, notes, report and self-update messages, and
a self-update rebuild's output) goes to stderr. See
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Debug logging.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Warnings and errors only.
.SH REPORT LIFECYCLE
The run store is the record; the pages are rendered from it. The order within
an invocation is fixed:
.IP 1. 4
phases 1 to 3 run and store their results;
.IP 2. 4
the run's final status (\fBcomplete\fR, \fBcomplete_with_failures\fR, or on
the failure paths \fBerror\fR or \fBinterrupted\fR) and
.I finished_at
are written;
.IP 3. 4
phase 4 records its
.B report
phase and an event against the run and assembles the narrative from the final
export;
.IP 4. 4
the run's bundle is rendered, the logbook entry is posted with that bundle's
pages attached, the logbook outcome is written into the bundle's
.I data/report.json
and recorded as an event, and only then is the site published, once.
.PP
So no page, JSON file or logbook entry of a finished run says it is in
progress. After an error or an interrupt the bundle is still rendered (never
posted or published) and carries the terminal status.
.PP
.B Current state is reconciled.
For each node and check the newest result wins. A failure that a later phase
re-checked and passed is listed as
.I resolved
(still visible in its phase's page, the evidence and the timeline); only
failures that are still current are
.IR outstanding .
Phase 2's profiles re-check subsets, so a phase-1 failure of a check phase 2
did not run again stays outstanding. A node's status is the roll-up of its
current checks, or UNREACHABLE when its latest assessment could not reach it.
The headline, counts, next steps, the logbook text and the exit status are all
derived from this one reconciled table, together with the latest phase-3
verdict.
.PP
.B Regeneration
.RB ( "\-\-phase report" ,
optionally
.BR "\-\-run\-id N" )
attaches to the selected run instead of opening a new one, never calls the
credential bootstrap, and never changes the run's status or
.IR finished_at .
It adds a
.B report
phase row and events to that run. Vault is contacted only when the logbook post
needs the ECL credentials; under
.B \-\-simulate
nothing is posted.
.PP
.B The report site.
Each run is rendered into
.I html/runs/<id>/
(pages and
.IR data/ )
from that run's stored rows alone, with a page only for the phases the run
contains; the navigation links only those. The directory is emptied first, so
nothing from another run can survive in it. The top level of
.I html/
is the latest view: the newest run's bundle rendered again, with the pages and
data files of phases the newest run lacks removed, plus
.I runs.html
(the run history). Regenerating an older run rewrites its bundle and leaves the
top level as it is.
.I report.keep_runs
bounds the number of bundles kept.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
With
.BR \-\-json ,
stdout carries exactly one JSON document and nothing else, so
.B "| jq ."
works directly; there are no ANSI escapes in it even with
.BR FORCE_COLOR .
For
.B \-\-list\-checks
and
.B \-\-list\-nodes
the document is the listing. Otherwise it is an object:
.PP
.RS
.EX
{"version":   {package_version, git_describe, ...},
 "run_id":    N or null,
 "status":    the run's stored status,
 "exit_code": the process exit status,
 "phases":    [one PhaseResult per phase that ran],
 "report":    {output_dir, run_dir, pages, data, latest,
               publication, ecl} or null,
 "error":     present only on an error or interrupt}
.EE
.RE
.PP
It is written on the error and interrupt paths too, with
.I error
set. Phase 4's full run export is not repeated in
.IR phases ;
it is in
.IR runs/<id>/data/run-export.json .
The four single-phase commands follow the same contract. An argument the parser
rejects is reported on stderr before any document exists.
.SH PHASE 0
A run that will contact the cluster begins by checking
.I origin
for a newer revision.
.B \-\-simulate
skips it, so a rehearsal cannot change the code it is rehearsing, and
.B \-\-list\-checks
returns before this phase is reached.
.B \-\-list\-nodes
does run it. The update is
.I fast-forward only:
if the working tree has local modifications, or the local branch has commits
the remote does not, the tool says so and runs the code that is checked out
rather than reconciling anything itself. A fetch that fails or times out is a
warning, never a stop. When an update is applied, the process re-executes once
so the new code is actually the code that runs.
.PP
If the update touched a build input
.RI ( selfupdate.rebuild_globs )
and the rebuild
.RB ( bootstrap.sh )
fails, is missing, or cannot start, the update is abandoned: the checkout is
reset to the full pre-update revision
.RB ( "git reset \-\-hard" ,
or
.B \-\-keep
when a dirty tree was allowed by
.IR selfupdate.allow_dirty ,
which keeps local edits), the process is
.I not
re-executed, and the run continues on the code it started with. The message
asks the operator to run
.B ./bootstrap.sh
by hand, and warns that a partly run bootstrap may already have changed the
virtual environment. If the reset itself fails the run stops with exit 2: the
checkout no longer matches the running code, which still imports modules from
disk. What phase 0 did (full before/after revisions, whether it rolled back,
the revision it attempted) is stored in the run's provenance
.RI ( version.selfupdate
in the run store and the report).
.SH RUN LOCK
An invocation that can act on hardware \(em phases 1, 2 or 3 without
.B \-\-simulate
\(em takes an exclusive operating-system lock on
.I run.lock_file
.RI ( logs/power-recovery.lock )
before phase 0, and a second such invocation exits 2 naming the holder's pid,
start time, host and command line.
.BR \-\-simulate ,
.BR \-\-list\-checks ,
.B \-\-list\-nodes
and report-only runs do not take it. The kernel releases the lock however the
process ends; the file, with the last holder's JSON record, is left in place
and believed only while the lock is held. The lock is released immediately
before the phase-0 re-exec and taken again by the new process.
.PP
.RS
.EX
python \-m mu2edaq_power_recovery.runlock status    # holder, or "not running"
python \-m mu2edaq_power_recovery.runlock pid       # pid only while held
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B stop\-mu2edaq\-power\-recovery.sh
signals only the pid the lock reports, and only while that process's command
line is still the driver's.
.SH DIAGNOSING A REFUSED LOGIN
The primary login and ticket are logged before anything is attempted, and each
attempt is logged at debug level in the form
.I "login X ticket Y [cache Z] \-> ok/refused"
\(em so a run that got in nowhere still says which identities it actually
offered. A gateway that refuses every credential lists each login/ticket pair
with its reason, rather than reporting only that the gateway did not answer,
and the refused pairs are recorded in the run store, so the report carries
them. Use
.B \-v
to see them as the run proceeds, and
.BR mu2e-ssh-probe (1)
to reproduce one by hand.
.PP
A
.I changed host key
is classified as its own kind of failure, not as an authentication failure, and
it stops the credential chain at the first attempt: no identity can get past
it, so offering the rest would only spend time. The
.I ssh.login
check reports that the host key has changed and that ssh refused before
authenticating. After an outage the usual cause is that the node was reimaged;
the alternative reading is an intercepted connection, so verify the new
fingerprint out of band \(em from a gateway,
.BI "ssh\-keyscan " node
\(em before anything updates
.IR ~/.ssh/known_hosts .
.PP
A refused connection, a connection reset, a
.I kex_exchange_identification
failure or "too many authentication failures" are read as
.IR unreachable ,
not as a credential problem: they are what a host that is down or rate-limiting
says, and they stop the chain for the same reason. Only an authentication
failure advances it. Once
.I ssh.login
has been refused, the remaining SSH checks for that node are skipped rather
than each opening its own connection to be refused again.
.SH LIVE RUNS
A run issues state-changing IPMI commands only when
.I this invocation
authorises it. The configuration can permit a live run; it cannot arm one.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Never live, whatever else is set.
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Live. This is the normal way. An
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ARM
token set alongside it is ignored, with a warning.
.TP
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ARM=\fIlabel\fP
For unattended runs that cannot pass a flag. Live only when the configuration
sets
.I run.dry_run: false
.B and
a non-empty
.I run.label
equal to the token. The token is read from the process environment alone: it
is not a configuration key, and putting it in
.I config/.env
is a configuration error (exit 2), because a persistent file that arms every
later run is exactly what it exists to prevent. A token with no label, the
wrong label, or with
.I run.dry_run
true exits 2.
.TP
.B run.dry_run: false alone
From the YAML file,
.I config/.env
or
.BR MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_RUN_DRY_RUN=false ,
with neither of the above: exit 2, with a message naming where the setting came
from and both ways to authorise.
.PP
The decision is made before phase 0 and before any credential. A live run
prints
.B "LIVE RUN \-\- power commands WILL be issued (authorised by ...)"
before the first phase, logs a warning and records an event in the run store
naming what armed it.
.SH PHASE 2 SCOPE
The power-on plan is made before any credential is acquired and before the
run is recorded; anything it cannot honour exactly exits 2.
.B \-\-location
drops stages in other locations (with a notice; none left is an error).
.B \-\-node
cuts the stages holding the named nodes to those nodes; the stages before them
(from the start of the
.BR \-\-from / \-\-until
range) are verify-only \(em power state read, ssh waited for, checks run, no
power command \(em and if one of them is not up, the run stops before the
requested nodes, even with
.BR \-\-continue\-on\-error ,
and says which stage to run explicitly. Stages after the last requested node
are not run. A name in no stage, or only outside the range or locations, is an
error naming its stage. See
.BR mu2e-power-on (1).
.SH TIME LIMITS
Four timeouts nest:
.TP
.I run.phase_timeout
Wall-clock budget of each of phases 1\(en3 (0 or null: unbounded). Checked
before every stage, node and check; what it never reached is UNKNOWN,
.IR "not run: phase_timeout expired" .
.TP
.IR boot_timeout " (per stage)"
How long phase 2 waits for a stage's nodes to answer ssh, all at once under one
stage deadline of now +
.RI min( boot_timeout ", phase time left)."
Concurrent ssh attempts are bounded by
.IR ssh.max_sessions .
.TP
.I ssh.command_timeout
Per command, capped at the phase time left.
.TP
.I ssh.connect_timeout
Per connection; a boot-wait attempt is
.I connect_timeout
+ 5 s, capped at the stage time left.
.PP
So a phase overruns its budget by at most the one call in flight when it
expires, and that call was itself capped at what was left when it started.
.SH SAFETY
.TP
.B Dry run by default
No power command is issued unless this invocation authorises it; see
.BR "LIVE RUNS" .
.B \-\-simulate
is the gate nothing can open: it forces
.I run.dry_run
back to true whatever else was asked for.
.TP
.B Scope is enforced twice
The phase-2 plan decides which hosts may be powered; the power step refuses any
other host independently and records it as
.IR out_of_scope .
.TP
.B Protected hosts
.BR "chassis power off" ", " cycle " and " reset
are refused for the gateways,
.IR mu2e-mgr-01 " and " mu2e-dcs-01 ,
regardless of
.BR \-\-execute .
Powering any of them down would cut the operator off from the cluster they are
recovering, or stop the NFS server everything else mounts from. The list is
.I protected:
in
.BR mu2edaq-topology.yaml (5).
.TP
.B A simulated run contacts nothing
Under
.B \-\-simulate
the SSH factory, the IPMI client
.I and
the local shell are all answered from the built-in script, so a check that
falls back to running a command locally \(em the gateways' own ICMP probe, for
one \(em cannot reach a real machine either. The publisher refuses before any
other test and reports that the report was written locally but nothing was
published: the publish step copies the tree directly rather than through a
transport, so scripting the transports would not have covered it.
.TP
.B One refused BMC credential stops IPMI
All the BMCs share a single credential set, so the first rejection settles the
matter. The run stops issuing IPMI commands and reports one diagnosis naming
the username that was refused and how to find the right one, instead of
walking the same wrong credential through all forty-five BMCs and locking the
account on each. Only a RAKP failure or
.I "unauthorized name"
counts as a rejection;
.I "Unable to establish IPMI v2 / RMCP+ session"
deliberately does not, because a dark chassis says exactly that and after an
outage a dark chassis is the expected case. This holds with sixteen workers
assessing at once: until one BMC has accepted the credential, IPMI commands go
out one at a time, so a wrong credential reaches one BMC, and every waiting
check reports the shared diagnosis as
.B unknown
without invoking ipmitool. Each such unproven call is preceded by one
.B ping \-c 1 \-W 1
of the BMC from the gateway, outside that one-at-a-time gate: a dark BMC is
reported unreachable without ipmitool, its
.BR power.sensors " and " power.sel
are
.B unknown
without a further call, and dark BMCs are therefore checked concurrently. Two
BMCs that answer the ping and still fail with "Unable to establish" trip the
stop as well (likely a wrong username).
.I ipmi.reachability_precheck: false
disables the ping for BMCs that filter ICMP. Set
.I ipmi.stop_on_auth_failure: false
to carry on regardless.
.TP
.B Credentials
BMC passwords are read from Vault and delivered to the gateway on standard
input, never as a command-line argument, so they appear in no process table on
either host. Kerberos passwords go straight to
.BR kinit (1)
on standard input and are not retained.
.TP
.B Audit trail
Every state-changing attempt, including every refusal, is written to the run
store before the command is issued.
.SH NOTES
The driver need not run on Linux. Where a probe's syntax differs by platform it
is chosen for the host that will actually execute it, not for the host the
operator is sitting at: iputils
.BR ping (8)
reads
.B \-W
as seconds, BSD
.B ping
reads it as milliseconds, and Windows spells the pair
.BR \-n / \-w .
Driving a recovery from a Mac therefore does not silently reduce a five-second
per-packet wait to five milliseconds, which would report a healthy gateway as
answering no ICMP. The jumbo-frame probe is the exception: it uses iputils-only
options, and only phase 3 asks for it, which always runs on a gateway.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config/power-recovery.yaml
Main configuration. See
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery.yaml (5).
.TP
.I config/topology.yaml
Node inventory. See
.BR mu2edaq-topology.yaml (5).
.TP
.I config/power-sequence.yaml
Phase-2 stage order. See
.BR mu2edaq-power-sequence.yaml (5).
.TP
.I config/checks.yaml
Check definitions, thresholds and profiles. See
.BR mu2edaq-checks.yaml (5).
.TP
.I config/.env
Local overrides. Not in version control.
.TP
.I data/power-recovery.db
The run store.
.TP
.I html/
The report site: the newest run's view at the top level,
.I runs.html
and one bundle per run under
.IR html/runs/<id>/ .
.TP
.I logs/power-recovery.log
Rotating log of every run.
.TP
.I logs/power-recovery.lock
The run lock
.RI ( run.lock_file );
see
.BR "RUN LOCK" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every configuration key can be overridden by an environment variable named
.BI MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ KEY
with the dotted path upper-cased and dots replaced by underscores, for example
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_SSH_CONNECT_TIMEOUT
for
.IR ssh.connect_timeout .
.B VAULT_ADDR
and
.B VAULT_TOKEN
are honoured in their conventional form.
.PP
Priority, lowest first: built-in defaults, the YAML config file,
.IR config/.env ,
the environment, the command line.
.PP
Four further variables are read directly rather than as configuration keys:
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to any non-empty value, output is not coloured, whatever the terminal
is.
.TP
.B FORCE_COLOR
When set, output is coloured even when stdout is not a terminal \(em for
capturing a coloured transcript into a file or a pipe.
.B NO_COLOR
wins if both are set.
.TP
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_ARM
The per-invocation live-run token; see
.BR "LIVE RUNS" .
Never read from
.IR config/.env .
.TP
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_UPDATED
Set to
.B 1
by phase 0 across its re-execution, so the new process does not check for
updates again. It exists to make an update loop impossible and is not intended
to be set by hand. Setting it does skip the check, like
.BR \-\-no\-self\-update ,
but the run reports it as having already updated rather than as disabled.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every phase completed and nothing failed.
.TP
.B 1
A phase completed but one or more nodes failed their checks.
.TP
.B 2
The run could not start: configuration, credentials, no reachable gateway, a
live run the invocation did not authorise, another run holding the run lock, a
failed update that could not be rolled back, a phase-2 stage or node selection
that cannot be honoured, or a
.B \-\-run\-id
that is misplaced or not in the store; or it stopped on an internal error
(recorded as status
.BR error ).
.PP
Statuses 0 and 1 are decided by the reconciled current state (see
.BR "REPORT LIFECYCLE" ),
so a failure that a later phase re-checked and passed does not make the
run exit 1.
.TP
.B 3
Interrupted by the operator.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read-only survey of the configured locations:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-recovery \-\-phase assess
.EE
.RE
.PP
Rehearse the whole four-phase run with no cluster attached:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-recovery \-\-phase all \-\-simulate
.EE
.RE
.PP
A real recovery, with a root principal and a label for the logbook:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-recovery \-\-phase all \-\-execute \e
    \-\-principal anorman@FNAL.GOV \e
    \-\-root\-principal anorman/root@FNAL.GOV \e
    \-\-label "Sept 2026 planned outage"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Resume a power-on that stopped at the
.I dcs
stage, after fixing it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power\-on \-\-execute \-\-from dcs
.EE
.RE
.PP
Regenerate and post the report for an earlier run (no new run is created):
.PP
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power\-report \-\-run\-id 17 \-\-post\-ecl
.EE
.RE
.PP
Machine-readable result:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-recovery \-\-phase all \-\-simulate \-\-json | jq .status
.EE
.RE
